# Trivy misconfiguration ignores for the AlgoT Kubernetes manifests.
#
# Used by .github/workflows/kubernetes-security.yml and by local runs:
#
#   deployment/scripts/render-placeholders.sh /tmp/rendered
#   trivy config /tmp/rendered --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
#     --ignorefile kubernetes/.trivyignore
#
# One entry, with its reason. Applicable findings are fixed in the manifests,
# not listed here. Several spellings of the ID are given because Trivy has
# changed the displayed form between releases and ignores IDs it does not
# recognise.

# KSV-0053: "Role should not have access to resource pods/exec".
#
# The algot-data-backfill-operator Role grants `create` on pods/exec. The
# check's rationale is that exec into a pod with an attached ServiceAccount
# holding higher RBAC permissions is a common escalation path to
# cluster-admin. That path does not exist here, and the precondition is
# enforced rather than assumed:
#
#   * Every pod in algot-data sets automountServiceAccountToken: false, and
#     so does every ServiceAccount in the namespace. There is no API token
#     inside any of these containers to steal, so a shell in one grants no
#     Kubernetes API access at all.
#   * deployment/scripts/validate-k8s-security.py fails the build if a pod
#     ever runs as the default ServiceAccount with a token mounted, or if a
#     Role in this namespace gains wildcard verbs, escalate/bind/impersonate,
#     or read access to Secrets. So this ignore cannot quietly become unsafe:
#     the change that would make it unsafe fails a different gate.
#   * The containers reached by exec are non-root, have a read-only root
#     filesystem, drop ALL capabilities and run under the restricted Pod
#     Security Standard, which the admission dry run in the same workflow
#     verifies against a real API server.
#
# pods/exec is required by the operational tooling rather than convenient:
# run-backfill.sh, run-database-integration-tests.sh, verify-deployment.sh,
# verify-network-policies.sh and assert-data-protection.sh all read state
# from inside the database container, which has no external listener beyond
# the ClusterIP API. Removing it would mean exposing the database or shipping
# a privileged sidecar, both of which are worse.
KSV-0053
KSV0053
AVD-KSV-0053
